<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taormina</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tauromenium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taormina is a hilltop town on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, above the bay of Naxos and facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-etna">Mount Etna</a>. Founded as Tauromenium in the 4th century BCE, with settlers including refugees from nearby Naxos, it prospered under Greek and Roman rule and is known for its ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. It was the last major Byzantine stronghold in Sicily, falling to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in 902.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taormina.jpg" alt="Taormina" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carsten Steger · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_image_of_the_coast_of_Taormina_(view_from_the_southeast).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
